Day 299/300: Flip Square Submatrix Vertically

Problem Statement #3643

Problem Statement Explanation:

Given a 2D integer matrix grid and three integers x, y, and k, perform a vertical flip on a square submatrix. 
The submatrix is defined by its top-left corner at coordinates (x, y) and has a side length of k.

A vertical flip means:

The first row of the submatrix is swapped with the last row of the submatrix.
The second row is swapped with the second-to-last row, and so on.
Elements within the rows maintain their relative horizontal order.

Solution Approach:

The solution employs a two-pointer row-swapping strategy confined within the submatrix boundaries:
Identify the Row Range: The submatrix rows range from index x to x+k−1.
Symmetric Swapping: We iterate through the first half of these rows (up to k/2).
Calculate Mirror Indices: For any row at offset i (where 0≤i<k/2), the target row to swap with is located at the bottom offset k−1−i.
In-place Column Swap: For each pair of rows, we iterate through the columns from y to y+k−1 and swap the elements.
This approach is efficient because it modifies the grid in-place without requiring additional matrix storage.

Here's the Code:

class Solution {
    public int[][] reverseSubmatrix(int[][] grid, int x, int y, int k) {
        for (int i = 0; i < k / 2; i++) {
            int row1 = x + i;
            int row2 = x + k - 1 - i;

            for (int j = y; j < y + k; j++) {
                int temp = grid[row1][j];
                grid[row1][j] = grid[row2][j];
                grid[row2][j] = temp;
            }
        }
        return grid;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(k^2)
Space Complexity: O(1)



